Provenance
----------

signal — clean-room implementation of the Signal Protocol's X3DH
(Extended Triple Diffie-Hellman) key-agreement, XEdDSA signature scheme,
and Double Ratchet, specified in three public documents published by the
Signal Foundation / Trevor Perrin (Double Ratchet also Moxie Marlinspike):

  - X3DH: "The X3DH Key Agreement Protocol", https://signal.org/docs/specifications/x3dh/
  - XEdDSA: "The XEdDSA and VXEdDSA Signature Schemes", https://signal.org/docs/specifications/xeddsa/
  - Double Ratchet: "The Double Ratchet Algorithm", https://signal.org/docs/specifications/doubleratchet/

Both are public specifications — a public spec's text is not copyrightable
expression (merger doctrine, CONVENTIONS.md §5); implementing it, however
closely, is not "derived from" any third party's code. This module's
scaffold pass (`x3dh.zig`'s DH+HKDF composition and wire codecs,
`xeddsa.zig`'s doc comments describing the sign/verify construction) was
built directly from the two specification texts above — no third-party
X3DH/XEdDSA implementation (libsignal, in any of its C/Java/Rust/Swift
incarnations; Signal's own Axolotl-era code; any other language's port)
was read, ported, or consulted as a design reference for the Zig code,
types, or API shape, and the later XEdDSA implementation pass filled the
already-transcribed construction in unchanged (its ONLY libsignal
consultation was test-vector sourcing and variant identification — see
"Test vectors" below). Every construction detail in `xeddsa.zig`'s doc
comments (the sign-0 point-forcing convention, the `hash1`-vs-`hash`
domain separation, the four-DH `F || DH1..DH4` framing, the HKDF
salt/info shape) was drawn from the specification text itself.

`std.crypto.dh.X25519` / `std.crypto.ecc.Edwards25519` / `std.crypto.hash.
sha2.Sha512` / `std.crypto.kdf.hkdf.HkdfSha256` /
`std.crypto.auth.hmac.sha2.HmacSha256` /
`std.crypto.aead.chacha_poly.ChaCha20Poly1305` (all MIT, part of Zig
itself) supply the DH, curve, hash, KDF, MAC, and AEAD primitives via
their public APIs; no new DH function, curve arithmetic, hash, KDF, MAC,
or AEAD primitive is implemented here — the specifications' OWN
composition of them (the four-DH agreement, the Montgomery<->Edwards
signature scheme, the root/chain KDF ratchets) is this module's
contribution, and the three Signal specifications are the sole references (no NOTICE-triggering third-party design reference exists for
this pass — CONVENTIONS.md §5's "pure clean-room from spec, no NOTICE
entry strictly required" case; this file is kept anyway per this
repository's established practice of a module-local NOTICE for
self-contained protocol/crypto modules, mirroring `oscore`'s precedent).

No GPL/LGPL/AGPL source was consulted or copied anywhere in this module.

Test vectors
------------

Neither the X3DH nor the XEdDSA specification text publishes a numeric
worked example (unlike, e.g., RFC 9383's Appendix C for SPAKE2+). The
X3DH agreement is therefore covered by a SELF-CONSISTENCY harness in
`src/kat_test.zig` (Alice's `initiateUnverified` and Bob's `respond`
must land on the identical `SK`/`AD` from independently-derived DH
computations — a property that would fail loudly on almost any
construction bug, even without an external oracle).

The Double Ratchet (`src/ratchet.zig`) is likewise covered by a
SELF-CONSISTENCY harness: the specification publishes no numeric worked
example, and no off-the-shelf byte-exact known-answer vector exists for a
clean spec-adherent instantiation (the reference implementations that ship
deterministic ratchet vectors — matrix's Olm/vodozemac, libsignal — use
their own protocol-specific AEAD/KDF instantiations, so matching them
byte-for-byte would mean re-implementing THEIR protocol, not the Double
Ratchet spec's own composition). No third-party Double Ratchet / Olm /
libsignal implementation was read, ported, or consulted as a design
reference for `ratchet.zig`; it was written directly from the Double
Ratchet specification text above, choosing ChaCha20-Poly1305 as the AEAD
(the spec permits any AEAD; its own example is AES-256-CBC + HMAC-SHA256)
and the spec's exact `KDF_RK` (HKDF) / `KDF_CK` (HMAC, 0x01/0x02) shapes.
Verification is Alice-and-Bob independently-advanced-ratchets agreement
across many DH ratchets, out-of-order delivery, `MAX_SKIP` DoS rejection,
and a tamper battery — see `SPEC.md`'s "Test-vector honesty".

That paragraph remains true of a whole MESSAGE, but it overstated the case
for the ratchet's KDF core, and `src/interop_vectors.zig` now closes that
gap. The Double Ratchet's `KDF_CK` is NOT application-defined: the spec
fixes it as `HMAC-SHA256(CK, 0x01) -> MK` and `HMAC-SHA256(CK, 0x02) -> CK'`,
so any faithful implementation agrees byte-for-byte, and libsignal does.
`KDF_RK`'s `info` string IS application-defined, but the rest of it (root
key as the HKDF-Extract salt with the DH output as IKM, a 64-byte Expand,
and the `RK' || CK` split order) is fixed too, and is checked against
libsignal by driving the production code path with libsignal's own `info`.
Both classes of divergence are invisible to an Alice-and-Bob harness —
demonstrated, not assumed: swapping `KDF_CK`'s 0x01/0x02, and inverting
HKDF-Extract's salt/IKM, each leave the ENTIRE pre-existing self-consistency
suite green and are caught only by these vectors.

Provenance and licence for those bytes: signalapp/libsignal at commit
`857c4dc`, crate `libsignal-protocol` 0.1.0, captured 2026-08-09 by
appending a local-only `#[cfg(test)]` driver to a throwaway checkout under
`~/.cache/zig-libs-signal/` (libsignal's `ChainKey`/`RootKey` are
`pub(crate)`) and printing the results — see `src/interop_vectors.zig` for
the exact command. libsignal is **AGPL-3.0**. No libsignal source is copied,
translated or vendored into this repository, `build.zig.zon` gains no
dependency, and the modified checkout is never redistributed; what is
recorded are numeric HMAC/HKDF outputs of a PUBLIC specification for fixed
inputs, each equally derivable from the spec text plus any HMAC/HKDF
implementation. This is the same posture, for the same reason, under which
this module already carries libsignal's XEdDSA known-answer vector below.
Note that the "No GPL/LGPL/AGPL source was consulted or copied" line above
has, since the XEdDSA pass, meant "copied" — reading was already disclosed
there and is disclosed here.

For XEdDSA, `src/kat_test.zig` embeds the known-answer vector from
libsignal's own test suite (`test_curve25519_signature` in
libsignal-protocol-c's `tests/test_curve25519.c`, GPLv3; the identical
bytes ship in libsignal-protocol-java): one private key, its public key,
one 33-byte message, and one 64-byte signature. These are NUMERIC FACTS
(a keypair and a signature over a fixed message), not copyrightable
expression — no libsignal code was ported, copied, or adapted. During
the XEdDSA implementation pass, libsignal's Rust `curve25519.rs`
(AGPL-3.0) and the C test file above were READ solely to (a) source that
vector and (b) establish the FACT — stated verbatim in the Rust file's
own doc comment — that deployed libsignal deviates from the XEdDSA paper
by carrying the signer's Edwards sign bit in the top bit of `s` instead
of forcing sign 0. This module's implementation itself was written from
the specification text (already fully transcribed into `xeddsa.zig`'s
doc comments during the scaffold pass, before any libsignal source was
looked at). `xeddsa.zig` now ships BOTH conventions as distinct, explicit
call sites: top-level `sign`/`verify` implement the PAPER's convention
unchanged, and the `xeddsa.libsignal` namespace implements the deployed
variant identified above — see `xeddsa.zig`'s module doc comment and
`SPEC.md`. Both `sign`s are additionally cross-checked (`sign` against
`std.crypto.sign.Ed25519`'s independent verifier, MIT, part of Zig; the
`libsignal.sign`/`verify` pair against each other and against the
libsignal vector) — no libsignal code was ported into either
implementation; only the numeric vector and the documented sign-bit fact
were taken from it.

Status of this pass
--------------------

**Part 1 + Part 2 complete.** `x3dh.zig`'s four-DH + HKDF agreement
(`initiateUnverified`/`respond`), both wire codecs (`PreKeyBundle`,
`InitialMessage`), the fail-closed `initiate`/`generateSignedPreKey`
entry points, `xeddsa.zig`'s `sign`/`verify` (spec variant) AND
`libsignal.sign`/`verify` (deployed-libsignal variant) — including
`edwardsFromMontgomery`, the Montgomery->Edwards sign-0 point recovery
std does not provide and both variants share — and `ratchet.zig`'s full
Double Ratchet (`State` + `initAlice`/`initBob`/`encrypt`/`decrypt`,
`KDF_RK`/`KDF_CK`, the DH + symmetric-key ratchets, `max_skip`-bounded
out-of-order handling, and a transactional fail-closed `decrypt`) are
implemented and tested — all green in Debug and ReleaseFast. Together
they form a usable Signal-style end-to-end-encrypted session, either
self-consistent (spec variant) or wire-compatible with real Signal
(`libsignal` variant). PQXDH (`src/pqxdh.zig`) implements the
post-quantum-augmented handshake Signal has defaulted to since 2023, over
ML-KEM-1024 rather than the round-3 Kyber the spec text names. Header
encryption (Double Ratchet spec §4), a PQXDH wire codec, and a
stability-committed session-persistence format remain out of scope — see
`README.md`/`SPEC.md`.

PQXDH's key-derivation vectors (`src/interop_vectors.zig`, `pqxdh_kdf`) are
a different and WEAKER provenance class than everything else in this file,
and are listed here so a distributor is not misled by their neighbours.
They are not libsignal's and not the protocol authors': Signal publishes no
byte-exact PQXDH vectors (checked 2026-08-22). They are the output of
`scripts/pqxdh-kdf-check.py`, written in this repository from Python's
`hmac`/`hashlib` alone — a second implementation of the same arithmetic, no
third-party code, no licence obligation of any kind. It catches a term in
the wrong order, a missing prefix, the wrong salt length; it cannot catch a
misreading of the spec shared by both implementations.
